1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Daniel Peter Layard was an English physician and midwife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Peter Layard was the son of Pierre Raymond de Layard (1666-1747), a Huguenot of good parentage of Monflanquin in Guienne (Lot et Garonne), who fled from France following the Revocation of the Edict of Nantes. Pierre Raymond
2 CONC  came to England as a member of the Corps of Noble Cadets of William III, and rose to the rank of Major in the British army in 1710, at which time he was in General Vezey's Regiment of Foot. Having become naturalized British in 17
2 CONC 13, he settled in Canterbury, and married, at the French Protestant chapel at the Savoy in the Strand, London in February 1715, to a much younger wife, Marie Anne la Croze or Croizett. They had several children, most of whom died 
2 CONC and were buried at St Alphege, Canterbury. Daniel Peter Delayard was born 28 March and baptized 8 April 1720 at the French Huguenot chapel of Des Grecs, Crown Street, Westminster. His father died in Westminster in 1747. His mother
2 CONC  lived down to 1773, and died testate in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Layard graduated Doctor of Medicine at Reims University on 9 March 1742. On 9 August of the following year (as Surgeon, of St Anne's Westminster), he married Suzanna Henrietta Boisragon at the Savoy chapel. In April 1747 he was ap
2 CONC pointed physician-accoucheur at Middlesex Hospital, but shortly afterwards he resigned on account of ill health and travelled abroad.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1750 Layard settled at Huntingdon, practising there for twelve years. On 3 July 1752 he was admitted a Licentiate of the College of Physicians (now the Royal College of Physicians of London). In c. 1762 he returned to London, w
2 CONC here he soon obtained an extensive practice as an accoucheur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Layard was physician to Princess Augusta of Saxe-Gotha, Princess, and Dowager Princess, of Wales. He was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society and a Fellow of the Royal Society of Sciences of Göttingen (now the Göttingen Academy o
2 CONC f Sciences and Humanities). Layard was a founder of the British Lying-in Hospital, of which he was later appointed a Vice President. On 20 June 1792 he was admitted to the degree of Doctor of Civil Law honoris causa at the Univers
2 CONC ity of Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Layard died at Greenwich in February 1802, leaving a will, and requested burial in a private crypt in St Benet's, Paul's Wharf, and that his late wife's remains should be brought there from their original burial-place at St Martin
2 CONC  in the Fields. He refers to the King's pleasure his wish that his unpublished manuscripts should be published for the financial benefit of his children; he refers particularly to the national services he has performed for the pre
2 CONC vention and cure of afflictions of black cattle in Great Britain and other European countries between 1769 and 1793, for which he anticipates reward or compensation of not less than £20,000; and he appoints Brownlow Bertie, 5th Du
2 CONC ke of Ancaster and Kesteven to be his executor.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Charles had become Dean of Bristol.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Daniel Peter Layard''' (1770-1802) 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth: 28 March 1720 at London, England 
2 CONT * Parents: Major Peter Layard and Marie Anne Crozé. 
2 CONT * Wife: Susanne Henriette de Boisragon, daughter of Lt.-Col. Louis Chevalleau de Boisragon and Marie Henriette de Rambouillet, on 9 August 1743. 
2 CONT * Death: 5 February 1802 at age 81 at London, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT * He graduated from Rheims University, Rheims, Champagne, France, in 1742 with a Doctor of Medicine (M.D.).
2 CONT *  He was Physaccoucheur in 1747 at Middlesex Hospital, London, England.
2 CONT * He was invested as a Fellow, Royal Society (F.R.S.).
2 CONT * He was registered as a Licentiate, College of Physicians (L.C.P.) in 1752.
2 CONT * He was Physician to Augusta, Princess of Wales.
2 CONT * He was awarded the honorary degree of Doctor of Civi Law (D.C.L.) by Oxford University, Oxford, Oxfordshire, England, in 1792.
2 CONT * He was invested as a Fellow, Society of Antiquaries (F.S.A.).
2 CONT * He wrote the book  The Bite of a Mad Dog.
2 CONT * He wrote the book  The Contagious Distemper Among the Horned Cattle in These Kingdoms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Daniel Peter Layard and Susanne Henriette de Boisragon
2 CONT * Very Rev. Charles Peter Layard b. 19 Feb 1748/49, d. 11 Apr 1803
2 CONT * Lt.-Gen. John Thomas Layard b. a 1750, d. 22 May 1828
2 CONT * Lt.-Gen. Anthony Lewis Layard b. 15 Jul 1750, d. 7 Jun 1823
2 CONT * Susanna Henrietta Layard b. 15 Sep 1759, d. 5 May 1832
2 CONT * Charlotte Sophia Layard b. 9 Jan 1762, d. Dec 1844
2 CONT
